TI  - Whose Knowledge Becomes the Tourism Story: Generative AI, Local Knowledge, and the Future of Tourism
AB  - This systematic review examines how Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) may influence local knowledge in tourism, focusing on algorithmic mediation, epistemic authority, and representational justice. Synthesizing 35 articles across four literature streams published primarily between 2023 and 2026, covering AI technologies, local knowledge systems, computational mediation, and cultural representation, the paper examines how conversational travel agents and large language models may influence destination storytelling. The synthesis indicates that GenAI can function as an active intermediary rather than simply as a neutral information channel. Eleven operational transformations are identified, including selection, translation, de-contextualization, recombination, and redistribution, through which local knowledge may be altered as it moves into AI-mediated tourism environments. The review suggests that these processes may reproduce existing differences in tourism knowledge visibility, particularly where AI systems rely heavily on digitally available and commercially prominent information while local, oral, or less digitally represented knowledge remains comparatively less visible. The literature also identifies concerns regarding AI-generated inaccuracies, including historical hallucinations and cultural simplification, and suggests that the fluency of AI-generated content may contribute to perceptions of authority among users. From a knowledge governance perspective, the synthesis raises concerns about the implications of extracting and processing traditional and Indigenous knowledge without adequate community participation, consent, attribution, or benefit-sharing, particularly in relation to Indigenous Data Sovereignty. Rather than concluding that GenAI inevitably displaces traditional knowledge authorities, the review suggests that it may introduce an additional layer of influence through which tourists encounter and evaluate destination knowledge. To address these concerns, the paper proposes a six-pillar Responsible AI Governance Framework encompassing cultural sensitivity, transparency, inclusivity, community-informed co-design, platform accountability, and respect for local ownership. The review concludes that responsible integration of GenAI in tourism requires greater attention to human oversight, community participation, knowledge governance, and the rights of local knowledge holders.
