TI  - Countering AI homogenisation: Developing adaptable writers through multi-genre pedagogy
AB  - As AI-assisted writing tools become an increasingly routine presence in higher education, concerns have emerged about the homogenisation of academic writing in terms of style, genre, and discipline. By outsourcing creativity and voice to LLMs—which have no access to the nuances of students’ writing contexts—we risk stifling our students and limiting their potential to develop into agile, adaptable writers, capable of deftly managing linguistic choices for specific research communities and genres. In this context, we propose a novel approach to teaching academic writing, known as multi-genre pedagogy (MGP). In MGP, we apply the principles for effective task design outlined by Swales and Feak (2023) to develop tasks that require students to recontextualise their writing for different audiences and purposes through varied genres. Rather than remaining tethered to prototypical fixed-genre models—which can be readily conjured up by LLMs—this pedagogy requires a human-centred approach, sensitive to the situated nature of writing and the specific institutional, disciplinary, and communicative contexts in which students operate. If AI risks promoting an homogenisation of writing, this approach is calibrated towards cultivating the opposite: stretching students to diversify their writing in response to the unfamiliar and evolving communicative demands of contemporary academia.Our project draws on the framework developed by Tardy et al. (2020), which conceptualizes genre knowledge and metacognition as key components of effective recontextualisation. We distinguish between genre-specific knowledge and genre awareness, an adaptable understanding of how genres function. These forms of knowledge interact with metacognitive processes, particularly writers’ ability to monitor and regulate their own strategies when navigating unfamiliar communicative demands. By foregrounding recontextualisation rather than static mastery, this framework supports a pedagogical shift from writing within genres to writing across them, an approach well-suited to the disciplinary and communicative challenges of the present moment.Our project draws from data collected from doctoral students in Sweden, the United Kingdom, the United States, and Norway. Drawing on student writing, classroom recordings, and interviews, we aim to assess both the pedagogical impact of MGP and the metacognitive processes it may activate. By comparing cases across national and institutional contexts, we seek to identify how MGP supports students in developing transferable writing strategies.We will share task examples and emerging insights from the initial stages of the project and reflect on the methodological challenges and opportunities of evaluating writing transfer in authentic classroom settings where AI-assisted tools increasingly form part of students’ writing environments. Rather than treating AI use as a variable to be controlled or an affordance to be exploited, the study de-centres LLMS and focuses on the role of teachers and their professional judgment in designing and sustaining pedagogical practices that foreground rhetorical choice and context-sensitive adaption. Ultimately, the project aims to inform L2 writing and EAP course design by demonstrating how multi-genre pedagogy can support the development of adaptable, rhetorically aware writers while maintaining writing as a meaningful human practice in evolving academic contexts.
