TI  - The Atom Test: Does Forecast Error Travel With the Model or the Agent? A Sixth Pre-Registered Test of the Homogenisation Mechanism
AB  - Sixth registered test of the homogenisation mechanism (companion to SSRN 7174958; prior registrations osf.io/gf82j, a8yk2, ev78k, r8hgy, w53cd — the last confirming post-2006 model homogenisation in East Pacific hurricane guidance on held-out data). This test targets the theory's foundational primitive directly: that an informed agent's error contains a bias shared with all users of the same interpretive model. In ForecastBench's open archive, named base LLMs (models) each run under multiple prompting scaffolds (users), on identical question sets with public resolutions, across 31 biweekly rounds. Primary hypothesis: holding the question fixed, same-base-model/different-scaffold forecast-error pairs correlate more than different-base-model/same-scaffold pairs. A null or negative result means the primitive fails its first direct test and will be reported as such. Gates were calibrated by a disclosed structure-only pilot (file enumeration and record counts; no forecast, resolution, or correlation touched); no deviation analysis is permitted. Prior art on cross-LLM correlation levels (Kim et al., ICML 2025; Spiro 2026) is distinguished in the document: the registered claim is the one-dimension-at-a-time contrast. The attached PDF governs; analysis begins only after this deposit.
